  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 118 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nPrecipitation Chances on Sunday:\n\nEnsemble guidance suggests the synoptic pattern described above\nwill begin to break down late Saturday into early next week. As\nit does so, there is consensus in a shortwave transiting the \nPlains, bringing modest moisture, lift, and precipitation with\nit. Cluster analysis shows there is meaningful non-consensus on\nthe track of this system, however. Canadian and European \nmembers tend to favor a more northerly track, which would mean \nprecipitation is more likely to get into southwest Missouri and\nsoutheast Kansas; GEFS members tend to favor a more southerly \ntrack, however, which would keep the area mostly if not entirely\ndry. Thus, NBM PoPs remain down the middle, ranging from 40-60%\nacross far southwest Missouri and southeast Kansas, with lower \nchances as you look toward central and eastern Missouri. It is \ndifficult to pick a \"winner\" scenario at this point, and \nmonitoring of forecast trends is prudent. Currently the highest \nprecipitation chances are Sunday afternoon into Sunday evening. \nShould this happen, temperatures will be warm enough to keep the\nprecipitation as all rain. If precipitation lingers into Sunday\nnight, however, temperatures near 32 degrees could allow for a \nfew snowflakes to mix in with the rain. For reference, NBM 25th \npercentile temperatures currently sit between 32 and 37 degrees.\nGiven these marginal temperatures and only modest QPF to begin \nwith, no accumulations or impacts are expected even if a few \nflakes do fall.\n\nGradual Warmup Next Week:\n\nGlobal models depict upper-level ridging taking hold over the \nPlains throughout the week. This pattern change will promote a \nsteady and gradual warmup each day, with temperatures \napproaching seasonal norms near 60 degrees by midweek (and \nlikely even higher through the week's end).",
